/*!
    \page guide-recipes.html
    \title Recipes
    \previouspage {Coding Conventions}

    Short step by step instructions for the changes people make most often.

    \section1 Add a creature

    \list 1
        \li Add an entry to \c data/creature_types.json with \c id, \c name, \c group (the
            race or a wildlife group), \c color and the stats \c health, \c attack,
            \c attackRange, \c visionRange and \c speed.
        \li Optional fields: \c markerColor for a small mark on the placeholder graphic,
            \c behavior (\c aggressive or \c fleeing), \c areaRadius and \c areaColor for area
            damage.
        \li If the creature has its own sprite sheet, add its id to the list in
            \c qml/components/CreatureSprites.js. Without that, it is drawn as a tinted
            placeholder box, which is fine for testing.
        \li To make a race able to produce it, add it to the \c units list of the race's
            base in \c data/base_types.json, see below.
    \endlist

    \section1 Let a base produce a unit

    Every entry in \c units of a base type has a \c creature, a \c tier and the \c gold,
    \c food and \c timeMs of one spawn. Tier 1 is available from the start. For higher tiers
    add a \c training object with the \c gold and \c timeMs of the research that unlocks the
    unit. A unit of tier N appears in the menu once the base reaches level N.

    \section1 Add a research path

    \list 1
        \li Add an entry to \c researches in \c data/tech_tree.json with an \c id, a \c name,
            an \c effect and a list of \c levels. Each level has \c baseLevel, \c gold,
            \c timeMs and \c value.
        \li Supported effects are \c baseRegeneration, \c baseHealthPercent, \c healingAura,
            \c baseDamagePercent, \c shotCount and \c baseRangeBonus. A new kind of effect needs
            a new \c research_effect_t value in \c backend/tech_tree.h and code in
            \c game_t::apply_base_stats or wherever the effect applies.
        \li Pick an icon for the research in the \c researchIcons map of
            \c qml/components/ActionCircle.qml. Without one, a plus icon is shown.
    \endlist

    \section1 Tune the balance

    Most balancing needs no code at all. Change the numbers in \c data and restart the game:
    base health and damage per level in \c base_types.json, unit stats in
    \c creature_types.json, costs and times in \c tech_tree.json and \c base_types.json,
    income in \c economy.json and AI behaviour in \c ai_profiles.json.

    \section1 Add a race

    A race is a base type plus its creatures. The data part is easy, but some code still
    lists the three current races by name:

    \list 1
        \li Add the creatures and a base type with a new \c group to the data files.
        \li Add a card for the race to the race list in \c qml/scenes/MenuScene.qml and a
            difficulty entry for it.
        \li Pass the difficulty of the new race in \c GameBackend::startMatch and in
            \c qml/Main.qml.
        \li \c game_t::start_match places exactly three match bases. A fourth race needs a
            fourth starting position there.
    \endlist

    Making races fully data driven is one of the planned improvements.

    \section1 Add a sprite sheet

    The quickest way is the import script, which packs the frames in the same format
    TexturePacker produces:

    \list 1
        \li Draw an animation strip of 96 by 128 pixels: 3 columns (idle and two walking
            frames) of 32 by 32, rows in the order north, east, south, west. Save it as
            \c {<id>_animation_96x128.png}. Add \c {<id>_corpse_32x32.png} for the corpse.
        \li Put both files into a folder named after the creature id, inside one source
            folder for all creatures.
        \li Run \c {python scripts/import_creature_sprites.py <source folder>}. It writes the
            packed sheet with extrude and padding, the JSON and the corpse in all three
            resolutions to \c {assets/creatures/<id>/}.
        \li Add the id to \c qml/components/CreatureSprites.js.
    \endlist

    If you pack the sheet with TexturePacker instead, use the JSON (Hash) format, frame
    names like \c south_1.png with rows north, south, west and east, one pixel of extrude
    and two pixels of padding, and export \c +hd and \c +hd2 at twice and four times the size.

    \section1 Regenerate the placeholder art

    The bases of all four levels and the placeholder creature come from a script:

    \badcode
    python scripts/generate_placeholders.py
    \endcode

    It draws grey boxes in the oblique projection and writes all three resolutions. The
    game tints them with the race colour at runtime.
*/
